## Southeast Asian History
@ Maritime trade | River-valley settlement | Cultural exchange | Colonial rule
Ports connect distant merchants across sea routes. Which historical process?|0|Shipping links regional economies.
Farming communities grow near reliable water and fertile soil. Which settlement pattern?|1|River resources support agriculture.
Religious ideas and writing spread through contact. Which process?|2|Interaction can transmit cultural practices.
A foreign state governs a territory without local sovereignty. Which relationship?|3|External political control characterizes colonial rule.
@ Philippines | Indonesia | Vietnam | Myanmar
Which present-day country experienced long Spanish colonial rule?|0|Spain colonized the Philippine archipelago.
Which present-day country includes the former Dutch East Indies?|1|The colony preceded modern Indonesia.
Which country was part of French Indochina?|2|Vietnam experienced French colonial control.
Which present-day country was known as Burma under British rule?|3|Burma became Myanmar in official naming.
@ ASEAN | 1967 | Bangkok | Regional cooperation
Which association's founding declaration was signed by five Southeast Asian states?|0|ASEAN emerged through that declaration.
In which year was the Bangkok Declaration signed?|1|ASEAN was founded in 1967.
Where was ASEAN's founding declaration signed?|2|Bangkok was the signing location.
What broad goal joins member-state dialogue and collaboration?|3|The association supports regional cooperation.
@ Primary source | Comparative history | Decolonization | Historical continuity
An independence-era proclamation is studied as original evidence. What source type?|0|It directly documents its period.
Colonial experiences in two countries are examined together. What approach?|1|Comparison identifies differences and shared patterns.
Colonial political control ends through independence movements. What process?|2|Decolonization changes sovereignty.
Trade networks persist despite changes in rulers. What concept fits?|3|Some patterns endure across political transitions.
@ Avoid a single regional stereotype | Distinguish independence paths | Evaluate colonial perspectives | Include local actors
Why cannot one empire's experience describe all Southeast Asian countries?|0|Regional histories are diverse.
Why compare negotiation, resistance and international circumstances separately?|1|Independence followed varied processes.
A colonial report calls every protest disorder. What should be examined?|2|Its institutional purpose can shape the account.
A narrative credits only foreign leaders for political change. What evidence should be added?|3|Local movements and communities acted historically.
## World History & Civilizations
@ Agriculture | Surplus | Specialization | Urbanization
Cultivation and animal domestication support settled food production. What development?|0|Agriculture changes subsistence practices.
Production exceeds immediate consumption. What is created?|1|Surplus can support storage and exchange.
People perform distinct occupations rather than all doing every task. What process?|2|Division of labor develops specialization.
Population and institutions concentrate in towns. What process?|3|Urbanization increases settlement concentration.
@ Mesopotamia | Egypt | Indus region | China
Which civilization developed between the Tigris and Euphrates?|0|Mesopotamia occupied that river region.
Which civilization was strongly associated with the Nile?|1|Nile resources supported Egyptian life.
Harappa and Mohenjo-daro belong to which early civilization region?|2|They were Indus urban centers.
The Huang He is associated with early civilization in which country?|3|The river lies in China.
@ Writing | Law code | Trade | Political authority
Records preserve transactions and information beyond memory. Which development?|0|Writing supports durable communication.
Rules and penalties are formally recorded. Which institution?|1|A code organizes stated legal rules.
Goods move between communities through exchange. Which activity?|2|Trade connects producers and consumers.
Institutions organize collective decisions and enforcement. What is exercised?|3|Political authority coordinates and controls public life.
@ Chronology | Causation | Continuity | Change
Placing events before and after one another establishes what?|0|Chronology concerns sequence.
Explaining why a development occurred examines what?|1|Causal analysis examines contributing conditions.
Identifying practices that persist across centuries examines what?|2|Continuity highlights endurance.
Comparing a society's institutions before and after a reform examines what?|3|Change identifies differences over time.
@ Multiple causes can interact | Sources reflect perspectives | Exchange includes ideas | Similar institutions need not have identical origins
Why is 'one invention caused civilization everywhere' too simple?|0|Environmental and social factors can combine.
Why compare rulers' monuments with household evidence?|1|Different sources illuminate different experiences.
Why study routes beyond the movement of merchandise?|2|Knowledge and beliefs can travel too.
Two societies have monumental buildings. What caution is needed?|3|Similarity alone does not prove a single origin.
## Philippine Politics & Governance
@ Legislature | Executive | Judiciary | Constitutional commissions
Which branch generally legislates national policy?|0|Congress exercises legislative authority.
Which branch implements laws through administration?|1|Executive institutions administer policy.
Which branch resolves legal disputes through courts?|2|Judicial power includes adjudication.
Which category includes independent constitutional bodies such as COA?|3|These bodies have defined constitutional mandates.
@ Accountability | Transparency | Participation | Rule of law
Officials must answer for public decisions and resources. What principle?|0|Authority carries answerability.
Relevant public information is accessible for scrutiny. What principle?|1|Disclosure supports oversight.
Residents meaningfully contribute to public consultation. What principle?|2|They take part in decision-making.
Public power is subject to applicable law. What principle?|3|Officials are not above legal constraints.
@ Checks and balances | Decentralization | Public interest | Conflict of interest
Different branches can restrain each other's powers. What arrangement?|0|Distributed authority permits reciprocal checks.
Some authority is exercised through local governments. What process?|1|Functions are distributed beyond the center.
A decision prioritizes community needs over an official's private gain. What standard?|2|Public office should serve the public.
An official's personal business may benefit from their decision. What issue?|3|Private interest may compromise public duty.
@ Constitution | Ordinance | Public policy | Civil society
Which is the fundamental national legal framework?|0|The Constitution structures public authority.
Which law is enacted by an authorized local legislative body?|1|Ordinances govern within local authority.
Which term describes an organized government course of action?|2|Policy addresses public concerns.
Which sphere includes voluntary nonstate civic organizations?|3|Civil society can participate outside government.
@ Examine actual powers and procedures | Compare outcomes with policy goals | Scrutinize evidence, not popularity alone | Preserve constitutional rights
An official claims unlimited authority because of electoral victory. What should citizens check?|0|Election does not remove legal limits.
A program spends its budget but misses its objective. What evaluation is needed?|1|Inputs alone do not establish effectiveness.
A widely shared accusation lacks documentation. What is appropriate?|2|Public judgment should evaluate credible evidence.
A majority demands punishment without a hearing. What constraint matters?|3|Popularity does not replace due process.
## Social Institutions & Stratification
@ Family | Education | Economy | Government
Which institution organizes many kinship and care relationships?|0|Families structure important social ties.
Which institution formally transmits knowledge and credentials?|1|Schools organize formal learning.
Which institution organizes production and exchange?|2|Economic institutions allocate goods and work.
Which institution organizes public authority?|3|Political institutions exercise collective power.
@ Social stratification | Social mobility | Social class | Social inequality
What is patterned hierarchical ranking of social groups?|0|Stratification orders access and standing.
What is movement between social positions?|1|Mobility concerns change in position.
Which category often groups people by economic resources and life chances?|2|Class concerns socioeconomic position.
What refers to unequal access to valued resources and opportunities?|3|Inequality concerns systematic differences.
@ Achieved status | Ascribed status | Role conflict | Role strain
A person earns a professional qualification. What kind of status?|0|It is acquired through activity and recognition.
A social position is assigned at birth. What kind of status?|1|It is not individually earned.
Student duties and employment duties clash. What tension?|2|Expectations across roles conflict.
Different demands within one caregiving role collide. What tension?|3|Strain occurs within a role.
@ Intergenerational mobility | Intragenerational mobility | Structural constraint | Institutional support
A person's class position differs from their parents'. What comparison?|0|It compares generations.
A person's economic position changes during their own lifetime. What comparison?|1|It follows one generation's trajectory.
Unequal school access limits options despite effort. What influence?|2|Social arrangements can restrict opportunity.
A scholarship expands access for disadvantaged pupils. What influence?|3|Institutional resources can widen opportunities.
@ Avoid blaming outcomes on effort alone | Distinguish averages from individuals | Examine intersecting disadvantages | Use comparable indicators
Why can identical effort lead to different educational outcomes?|0|Resources and constraints also differ.
A group has lower average income. What inference must be avoided?|1|Its average does not describe every member.
Gender, disability and poverty combine to limit access. What should be analyzed?|2|Disadvantages can interact.
Two inequality reports use household and individual incomes. What must be checked?|3|Different units affect comparability.
## Social Sciences & Social Theories
@ Sociology | Anthropology | Political science | Economics
Which discipline centrally studies social relationships and institutions?|0|Sociology examines organized social life.
Which discipline studies human cultural and biological diversity across contexts?|1|Anthropology considers varied human experience.
Which discipline examines power, governance and political institutions?|2|These are central political-science concerns.
Which discipline studies choices involving scarce resources?|3|Economics analyzes allocation and exchange.
@ Structural functionalism | Conflict theory | Symbolic interactionism | Social construction
A theory examines how institutions contribute to social continuity. Which perspective?|0|Functionalism considers interdependent social functions.
A theory emphasizes unequal power and competing group interests. Which perspective?|1|Conflict analysis examines inequality and struggle.
A theory focuses on meaning made in everyday interaction. Which perspective?|2|Interactionism studies symbolic social processes.
A category's meaning is produced through social practices. Which concept?|3|Shared activity can construct social meanings.
@ Observation | Interview | Survey | Document analysis
A researcher systematically watches interactions. Which method?|0|Observation records behavior in context.
A researcher asks participants open questions in conversation. Which method?|1|Interviews elicit participants' accounts.
A structured questionnaire gathers comparable responses. Which method?|2|Surveys standardize questioning.
A researcher examines archived meeting records. Which method?|3|Documents supply recorded evidence.
@ Theory | Hypothesis | Concept | Operational definition
What is an explanatory framework linking social ideas?|0|A theory organizes explanations.
What is a testable proposed relationship?|1|A hypothesis can be examined using evidence.
What is an analytical idea such as social status?|2|Concepts identify phenomena for inquiry.
What specifies how an idea will be measured?|3|Operational definitions make observation concrete.
@ Match explanation to evidence | Recognize levels of analysis | Separate explanation from endorsement | Compare theories' strengths and limits
Why should an attractive theory be revised when evidence repeatedly challenges it?|0|Explanations must respond to findings.
Why may an individual story not explain a national institutional pattern?|1|Different scales require suitable evidence.
Does explaining prejudice's social origin mean approving it?|2|Understanding a process is not moral endorsement.
Two theories explain different parts of one event. What is a productive response?|3|Their explanatory scope may differ.
## Sikolohiyang Pilipino
@ Virgilio Enriquez | Kapwa | Filipino language and culture | Indigenous perspective
Who is centrally associated with the development of Sikolohiyang Pilipino?|0|Enriquez advanced the movement.
Which concept concerns shared identity or personhood with others?|1|Kapwa is central to this orientation.
What grounds the approach in lived Filipino experience?|2|Language and culture inform its concepts.
What orientation develops understanding from within a community's experience?|3|Indigenization values local categories and meanings.
@ Pakikiramdam | Pakikipagkapwa | Contextual understanding | Reflexivity
Attending sensitively to interpersonal cues illustrates what?|0|Pakikiramdam involves relational sensitivity.
Relating to others with recognition of shared personhood illustrates what?|1|Kapwa informs interpersonal conduct.
Interpreting a response through its social setting illustrates what?|2|Meaning depends on situation and relationship.
A researcher examines how their own assumptions affect inquiry. What practice?|3|Reflexivity examines the researcher's position.
@ Language appropriateness | Respectful relationship | Participant understanding | Community relevance
Why avoid an unexplained foreign questionnaire term?|0|A measure needs understandable wording.
Why should inquiry avoid treating participants as mere objects?|1|Respect matters in research relationships.
Why check how participants interpret the questions?|2|The intended meaning may not be shared.
Why connect research questions with locally meaningful concerns?|3|Relevance supports useful grounded inquiry.
@ Indigenous concepts | Imported categories | Collaborative interpretation | Ethical consent
Locally meaningful terms guide analysis. What resource is used?|0|The community's concepts shape understanding.
An external model is applied without adaptation. What needs scrutiny?|1|Its categories may not fit local meanings.
Participants help clarify a researcher's interpretation. What practice?|2|Their knowledge informs analysis.
Participation follows understandable information and voluntary agreement. What safeguard?|3|Consent must be meaningful and voluntary.
@ Avoid essentializing all Filipinos | Evaluate borrowed models critically | Preserve local meaning in translation | Combine cultural grounding with evidence
Why cannot one community's pattern define every Filipino person?|0|Filipino experiences are diverse.
Does an indigenous approach require rejecting every idea developed elsewhere?|1|Appropriateness should be assessed rather than presumed.
A local term loses relational nuance in translation. What should be protected?|2|Literal wording may not carry its full meaning.
Why is cultural familiarity alone insufficient to establish a psychological claim?|3|Claims still need sound methods and supporting evidence.
